Longwave vhf-gamepad kit: sleep (S3) on Windows 11 bare metal (gaming-pc), 2026-10-05
================================================================================
Machine: gaming-pc hardware (Ryzen 5 5600X, RTX 3080, MSI B550M), Windows 11 Pro 26H2 build 26300,
host DESKTOP-M0A8FO0, HVCI on, SAC evaluation, Secure Boot on. Times are the PC's local clock (UTC-7).
Sleep states (powercfg /a): Standby (S3), Hibernate, Fast Startup. No S0 Low Power Idle (Modern Standby),
so "sleep" is S3. Wake timers allowed on AC. CrashControl\AutoReboot = 1 (unchanged).
Driven over SSH; each sleep test ran from a one-shot SYSTEM scheduled task (run-sleep-test.ps1 ->
lwpad-test sleep <mode> 45): the test arms a resume-capable waitable timer for 45 s, calls
SetSuspendState(sleep), and writes every line through to disk as it goes.

Sections:
  A. Root cause, from the live kernel dump of the first crash (ResourceTimeout-20261005-0455.dmp)
  B. Before the fix (driver as of 8cb1120): none = OK, idle = 0x9F bugcheck
  C. After the fix (DriverVer 0.1.0.7001): none, idle x2, churn x2, aware x2 = 0 FAIL
  D. XInput zeros while the console session is locked
  E. Raw logs (B and C above)
  F. Standard suite (run-gamepad-spike.ps1) on the fixed driver: 57 PASS 0 FAIL 0 SKIP

Summary
  mode   pads during the sleep                          before fix            after fix
  none   none (driver installed)                        OK                    OK
  idle   Xbox + DualSense, created then left alone      HANG -> 0x9F_3 (5 min) OK x2, both pads ERROR_DEVICE_REMOVED, recreated 2-8 ms after resume
  churn  idle pads + 3rd pad create/destroy ~70/s       not run (idle already hangs; churn always has a pad) OK x2 (204, 217 cycles across the sleep)
  aware  churn, plus the feeder destroys its pads on
         PBT_APMSUSPEND and recreates after resume       -                     OK x2 (destroyed in 0.7-16 ms; driver had nothing left to delete)

############ A. Root cause (cdb 10.0.26100.6584 on the 1.4 GB live dump taken 3 min into the hung sleep)
!poaction: Action Sleep, Lightest State Sleeping3, Irp minor SetPower. Pending power IRPs for PDO
  ffffbe02aabe75a0 (ROOT\LONGWAVEVIRTUALGAMEPAD\0000): set/S3 and set/D3, both CURRENT at \Driver\vhf.
Thread ffffbe02adefc040 (System), the D3 IRP being processed in vhf.sys's KMDF FDO, blocked:
    nt!KeWaitForSingleObject
    Wdf01000!FxIoQueue::StopProcessingForPower+0xf6
    Wdf01000!FxPkgIo::StopProcessingForPower+0x12f
    Wdf01000!FxPkgPnp::PowerGotoDx+0x2a
    ...
    Wdf01000!FxPkgFdo::DispatchDeviceSetPower+0x7c
    nt!IopPoHandleIrp / IofCallDriver
    WUDFRd!RdPnpTracker::RdPnpForwardToLowerDevice+0x80       (our UMDF filter had already passed it down)
!wdfdevice (vhf FDO): Power state WdfDevStatePowerGotoDx, PwrPol SleepingNoWakePowerDown, power policy owner,
  pended device power irp (D3) and system power irp (S3). The HID child PDO had already reached WdfDevStatePowerDx.
!wdfqueue 0x000041fd521095e8 (vhf FDO default queue): Parallel, Power-managed, PowerStoppingDriverNotified,
  "Number of driver owned requests: 1 ... cancelable ... !irp 0xffffbe02b0819ba0", EvtIoDeviceControl
  vhf!FDO_EvtIoDeviceControl, NO EvtIoStop.
!irp ffffbe02b0819ba0: IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL 0xb0336, pending in \Driver\vhf, issued by our WUDFHost.exe thread
  2ba4.19dc (start address in \Windows\System32\VhfUm.dll = VhfUm!VhfaAsyncIoctl::WorkerThread), which waits on
  2 objects (the IOCTL's event and its stop event).
Disassembly: FDO_EvtIoDeviceControl routes 0xB0336 to vhf!PDO_PullRequestNotify -> PullRequestLoop_ProcessNotifyRequest,
  which, when no HID output/feature request is waiting, calls WdfRequestMarkCancelableEx and keeps the request. It is
  completed only when a HID pull request arrives (PullRequestLoop_HidPullRequestQueueReadyNotification) or when it is
  cancelled (STATUS_CANCELLED). Nothing completes it on power-down. => every live pad parks one such request, so
  vhf.sys's D3 can never finish while a pad exists: 0x9F_3 after the 5-minute watchdog.
Our own queue (sequential, power-managed) held only lwpad-test's destroy IOCTL (0x22e008), not yet dispatched.
Why deleting pads in our EvtDeviceD0Exit is safe (also from the disassembly):
  VhfUm!VhfDelete -> VhfaPump::Stop for both pumps (sets the stop event; the worker calls CancelIoEx on its pending
  IOCTL, vhf completes it as cancelled) -> VhfaDevice::SendDeleteIoctl. vhf.sys handles the delete in
  FDO_EvtIoInCallerContext -> FDO_DeleteVhfDevice -> WdfPdoMarkMissing, i.e. in the caller's context, not through
  its power-managed queue, and without waiting for PnP to remove the child.
vhf.sys 10.0.26100.8972, WUDFRd.sys 10.0.26100.9444.

############ B. Before the fix (unfixed driver, built from 8cb1120 driver.cpp, DriverVer 0.1.0.6018)

Note on B-idle: the log ends at PBT_APMSUSPEND because the PC never finished going to sleep. SSH still answered
2 minutes in (user mode keeps running during the device phase); pads and device node showed OK. At 06:03:59 (5 min
after SetSuspendState) the PC bugchecked: WER 1001 "0x0000009f (0x3, 0xffffba83df263cc0, 0xfffff806303fe650,
0xffffba83dc20a010)", minidump 100526-37000-01.dmp: DRVPOWERSTATE_SUBCODE 3, FAULTING_MODULE vhf,
FAILURE_BUCKET_ID 0x9F_3_POWER_DOWN_IMAGE_ntkrnlmp. Unlike the first crash (04:57, rebooted by itself in ~40 s),
this time the PC did NOT restart and did not answer Wake-on-LAN; it stayed off the LAN for ~35 minutes until the
user powered it on (boot 06:39:33). AutoReboot was already 1, so the setting is not why. Probably the crash came
while devices were already powered down for S3 and the reset never completed; not investigated further.

